WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.878
[upbeat music]

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.676
(Speaker)
The Yale Peabody Museum,

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:14.055
I really think is an icon
for the city of New Haven,

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:16.725
for the state of Connecticut
and for New England.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:21.938
It's where so many people
and I count myself as well,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.233
learned about
the world around us,

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:30.113
understood our
place in the universe.

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:32.699 align:start size:3% position:97% line:0%
♪

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.961
(Mariana)
Fossil collections
at the Peabody are amazing.

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:46.129
They just are.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:48.715 line:76%
But also,
we have a lot of firsts,

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:50.467 line:76%
and not a lot of museums
can say that.

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.385 line:76%
We have a lot of
what are called holotypes.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:53.928 line:78%
So it's the first specimen

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:57.766
ever found
of that dinosaur, for example.

00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:00.685
And that is, first of all,
very exciting.

00:01:00.685 --> 00:01:03.188
And second,
you start thinking about it

00:01:03.188 --> 00:01:06.357
and it's every single other one
that has ever been found

00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:09.110
has been described
based on our collection.

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:18.286 line:78%
One of the key

00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:21.372 line:78%
parts of this whole project

00:01:21.372 --> 00:01:24.250 line:78%
is the Fossil Hall.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.630
Burke Hall is the heart
of the Peabody Museum.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.882 line:0%
<i>[wrench sound]</i>

00:01:31.132 --> 00:01:34.886
Some of the first ever
fossil mounts

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:38.264
are being reworked
to be displayed to,

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:41.226
you know, the next generation
of museum visitors.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:45.271
(Vanessa)
With regard to our upcoming
renovation due to open in 2024,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.857
we've had the fortune of
working with

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:49.776 line:78%
Research Casting International

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.237 line:76%
who's based up in
Trenton, Ontario, Canada,

00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:56.533 line:76%
and they're a professional firm
who brings to us expertise

00:01:56.533 --> 00:02:00.286
and skill in mounting specimens,
particularly

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:04.916
the very large mounts
that are here at the Peabody.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.877
They helped us
with deinstallation,

00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:11.923
removing these large specimens
from their original mount

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:15.677
posture in the hall,
and also with the conservation.

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:19.556
We're working with them
to revise the pose

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:23.017
as well as integrate
new materials

00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:26.479
into these mounts to make them
the most complete possible.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:28.898
The reason
why we want to do this is,

00:02:28.898 --> 00:02:31.150
first of all,
we want to update our knowledge.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.863
The science doesn't stop,
and so we want to be updated.

00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:37.240
Second, all materials age,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.535
and so I think we came in
at the right time

00:02:40.535 --> 00:02:42.745
to do a lot of this work,
a lot of this treatment

00:02:42.745 --> 00:02:45.290
and preparation
to prevent any damage

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.376
that could have happened
in the future.

00:02:48.376 --> 00:02:52.255 line:0%
[tranquil music]

00:02:56.718 --> 00:02:58.177
(Speaker 1)
Trenton, Ontario

00:02:58.177 --> 00:03:02.056
is a small town about an hour
and a half east of Toronto.

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:03.558
We're located
on the Bay of Quinte,

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:07.145
which is kind of the gateway
to the Trent-Severn Waterway.

00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:09.188 align:start size:69% position:31%
<i>[stream water running]</i>

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:11.024
(Speaker 2)
Very scenic.

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.401
A lot of tourists
come here in the summer.

00:03:14.736 --> 00:03:18.615
It's away from the sound and
the hub of Toronto, we're like a

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:20.199
nice, quiet community.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.328
(Speaker 1)
It is a bit of a
contrast what we do in here

00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:25.538
and what the scenery
is like outside.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:28.207
It's like a boating
and kind of vacation area

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:30.168
[heavy rock music]
[machine noise]

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.421
and then we have heavy
steel work going on

00:03:33.421 --> 00:03:36.257 align:start size:3% position:97% line:7%
♪

00:03:36.257 --> 00:03:39.385
and giant dinosaurs
and all sorts of creatures.

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:41.554
A lot of people are surprised
when they hear

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:42.764
what we do in here.

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:45.475
I don't think anybody realized
what we actually do.

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:47.226 align:start position:0% line:50%
[welder sparking]

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:47.226 align:start size:3% position:97% line:7%
♪

00:03:49.145 --> 00:03:51.189
We are Research
Casting International.

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:56.277 line:76%
Right now we're about 50,000
square feet, 40 employees

00:03:56.277 --> 00:03:59.572 line:76%
and we've been in business
35 years.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:02.200
Companies that do what we do,
you probably

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.576
count them on one hand.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:04.869
Of all the companies
in the world

00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:07.288
that do this sort of thing,
we're probably the largest.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.207
(Matt)
For the Yale Peabody project

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:11.501
we're doing
a number of specimens

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.962
which are pretty amazing,
like this archelon

00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:14.963 line:78%
we've been looking at

00:04:14.963 --> 00:04:18.257 line:76%
for a long time it's
one of our favorite specimens

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:19.050 line:78%
and one of mine

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.220 line:76%
specifically is the brontosaur
that we're doing and you know,

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.097
having things
that were collected by Marsh

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:25.765
and getting to work on them

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:28.601
are a pretty amazing thing
to be able to do.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.645
It's pretty iconic I think.

00:04:30.645 --> 00:04:35.984 line:76%
It's just because we get to work
on these massive creatures and

00:04:35.984 --> 00:04:38.444 line:78%
the brontosaur itself is,

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.446
when I think of a dinosaur,
that's the first dinosaur

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:42.532
I think of and a lot of kids
feel that way.

00:04:42.532 --> 00:04:44.450
We're very
excited to work on it.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.577
It's pretty cool work.

00:04:46.911 --> 00:04:49.872
[upbeat rock music]

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.376
When we start a project,

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:56.254
we do all the cleaning,
conservation, preparation first.

00:04:56.254 --> 00:04:58.297
Each person
has their own station

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:01.968
and they work on an individual
component of a specimen.

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:04.679
We usually start off
by cleaning the dinosaur bones

00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:08.016
with chemicals like acetone,
ethanol and sometimes alcohol.

00:05:08.016 --> 00:05:09.517
So we start by removing

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:11.477
any of the grime or dust

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.271
that has been built up
on the specimens

00:05:13.271 --> 00:05:17.025
and then sometimes we use an air
scribe to remove old plaster

00:05:17.025 --> 00:05:18.526
that's been covering the bones.

00:05:18.526 --> 00:05:21.612
There's lots of steps involved,
and then that would go

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:23.072
to the blacksmiths.

00:05:23.072 --> 00:05:26.034
<i>[machine noise]</i>

00:05:26.576 --> 00:05:28.745
(Brian)
Once the bones
come from the preparators

00:05:28.745 --> 00:05:30.788
and they're all cleaned
and consolidated,

00:05:30.788 --> 00:05:33.207
they store them
in the storage area in our room

00:05:33.207 --> 00:05:37.503
and we move them to our
sandbox as you can see here.

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.922
They're supported in the sandbox

00:05:39.922 --> 00:05:43.509
and then we start measuring
for an armature.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:48.014 line:76%
Obviously, this being a
historically significant fossil,

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:51.601 line:76%
we have to take a lot of care
to preserve it and preserve all

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:55.063
the numbering and markings on it
and the history behind it.

00:05:55.063 --> 00:05:56.898
These armatures
protect the bone.

00:05:56.898 --> 00:05:58.900
It also doesn't
destroy any of the fossil.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.068
It's noninvasive,

00:06:00.068 --> 00:06:02.904
and you can remove any bone
from the mount that way.

00:06:02.904 --> 00:06:05.573 line:0%
[lift machinery]
beep, beep, beep

00:06:05.573 --> 00:06:07.033
After all the armatures
are done,

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.535
we build the
support steel for it

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.079
and we start building the pose.

00:06:11.079 --> 00:06:14.707
So all of these armatures
get welded together

00:06:11.079 --> 00:06:14.707 align:start size:75% position:25% line:50%
[sound of welder sparks]

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.626
into the desired pose.

00:06:16.626 --> 00:06:18.920
It's going to look
a lot more dynamic.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:21.756
It looks more in motion
than it did before.

00:06:21.756 --> 00:06:25.343
(Vanessa)
We want the specimens
to be interesting and lifelike,

00:06:25.343 --> 00:06:26.803
even though they're static,

00:06:26.803 --> 00:06:29.806
and so that can just take
very small changes

00:06:29.806 --> 00:06:32.058
in the way that the head
is positioned

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:34.727
or that the tail
is bent and turned.

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.146
This is revised by new research

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:39.857
and it's informed
by new collections.

00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:42.527
We try to find fossils
and increase the data set

00:06:42.527 --> 00:06:45.279
available to us
to study and interpret

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:47.824
how an organism
may have changed through time,

00:06:47.824 --> 00:06:49.992
what its paleoenvironment
may have been like,

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:52.662
what its locomotion
may have been like.

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:56.040
[warm relaxing music]

00:06:58.376 --> 00:07:02.588 align:start position:0%
(David)
All of the tremendous science
that's been done over the past

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:07.468 align:start position:0%
many decades is informing
what we're able to do for this

00:07:07.468 --> 00:07:09.971
this next generation of display.

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:12.223
We've got great partners
who are helping us do that.

00:07:12.223 --> 00:07:16.185
Working with RCI has been a
really smooth experience for us.

00:07:16.185 --> 00:07:21.607
Their approach to their work
is one of the highest standards

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:25.111
when it comes to the materials
and the techniques that they use

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:28.656
and the care that they bring
to our materials.

00:07:28.656 --> 00:07:32.201
We as an institution
have entrusted RCI with

00:07:32.201 --> 00:07:35.997
our most precious specimens,
and they in return

00:07:35.997 --> 00:07:39.500
are really taking this material
to the next level

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.461
and presenting it to the world
in such a way

00:07:42.461 --> 00:07:46.841
that it will be enjoyed and
learned from for years to come.

00:07:46.841 --> 00:07:49.844
Knowing that the work that
I'm doing now is going to be up

00:07:49.844 --> 00:07:51.554
for another century.

00:07:51.554 --> 00:07:56.893
Is, it's pretty humbling that
I've been given this opportunity

00:07:56.893 --> 00:08:01.022
to work on such a historically
significant animal and

00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:05.651
it's pretty amazing that
my work is going to outlast me.

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.612
I just hope that 90 years
from now,

00:08:07.612 --> 00:08:10.031
people like me
and paleontologists

00:08:10.031 --> 00:08:13.534
will look at it and be like
they did a good job.

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:18.664
(Peter)
Working on the Yale Peabody
project has been a blessing.

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.250
Yeah, it's been a really,
really nice project to work on.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:23.294
This was the beginning
of paleontology

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:24.253
and these specimens

00:08:24.253 --> 00:08:25.963
that we're working on
were the specimens

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:29.175
that started paleontology
on that road.

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:33.012
All the history behind it all,
in my mind, it's just amazing.

00:08:34.055 --> 00:08:37.892 align:start size:3% position:97% line:7%
♪

